import { QURAN_CORPUS, attested } from "./quran-corpus.js";

/**
 * Three-letter Quranic words, chosen and glossed by hand, arranged into cards.
 *
 * WHERE THESE WORDS COME FROM
 *
 * quran-corpus.js is generated by scripts/mine-quran-words.mjs, which sweeps
 * the whole Uthmani text for words of exactly three letters where each letter
 * carries exactly one fatha, kasra or damma — the deck's own alphabet of marks,
 * and nothing more. There are 230 of them. This file picks about seventy of
 * those and says what they mean.
 *
 * The split matters. Nothing here can name a word the corpus does not attest:
 * unattested() lists any that slip through and the tests fail on a non-empty
 * result. So the answer to "are these proper Arabic words" is not my judgement
 * or a dictionary's uptime — it is that every one of them is in the mushaf, at
 * the ayah printed on the card.
 *
 * WHAT IS STILL A HUMAN'S JOB
 *
 * The English glosses below are NOT machine-checked, and cannot be. They are
 * the short classroom sense of the word, not a translation of the ayah it sits
 * in, and a three-letter Arabic verb usually carries several senses this one
 * line does not attempt. Each card shows its ayah reference for exactly this
 * reason: an ustazah who disagrees with a gloss can see the verse it came from
 * and correct the line here.
 *
 * HOW THE CARDS ARE BUILT
 *
 * Following the deck's own design rather than inventing a new one. The printed
 * cards teach by minimal contrast — بَنَتَ beside بُنِتَ, the same letters with
 * the harakat moved — because that is what forces a child to read the marks
 * instead of recognising a shape. Six cards here do the same thing with real
 * words, pairing an active verb with its attested passive: خَلَقَ / خُلِقَ,
 * كَتَبَ / كُتِبَ, وَعَدَ / وُعِدَ. Both halves are in the Quran, so the child gets
 * the deck's exercise and a word worth knowing from the same card.
 *
 * The rest group by sense, so that a card is also a small piece of vocabulary:
 * the words of the heart, the words for coming and going, four things you can
 * point at.
 *
 * WHAT IS LEFT OUT, AND WHY
 *
 * Most of the 230. Three kinds are excluded on purpose:
 *
 *   1. Words that are a particle or pronoun stuck to something else —
 *      وَهُوَ, لَكُمُ, مَعَهُ, فَقَدِ. They are the most frequent hits in the
 *      corpus and the least use: "and he" is not vocabulary, and a child who
 *      learns وَلَهُ as a word has learned a seam, not a word.
 *   2. Verbs carrying a liaison vowel that only exists because of the next
 *      word — يُطِعِ, لَقَدِ, يَجِدِ. The final kasra is a joining artefact, so
 *      the card would be teaching a reading that does not survive on its own.
 *   3. Vocabulary that is real, Quranic, and not for a six-year-old to read
 *      aloud in class: the verbs of killing, cursing, slaughter and marital
 *      relations, and Hellfire by name. No filter can make that call, and the
 *      expletive screen in clean-words.js is not trying to — it catches
 *      accidental obscenity in other languages, not heavy meanings in this
 *      one. This is the judgement call, and it is deliberately a human's.
 *
 * A teacher who wants any of those can add it: the corpus lists every one with
 * its reference, and adding a line here is all it takes.
 */

